namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup reduction

\section reduction-intro Introduction

Reduction operations consist in computing a single value from an array.

\section reduction-single Single-array reductions

Linx provides several basic reduction operations such as sum or min,
and supports custom reductions using the `reduce()` function.
The reducer must be a diadic function for which `identity_element(reducer)` is defined.
The library comes with many compatible functors, like `Add`, `Multiply` or `Max`.
See the `identity_element()` overloads at the bottom of this page for a complete list.
Many builtin reduction functions like `sum()`, `product()` or `max()` are also listed.

\snippet{trimleft} reduction_example.cpp single

It is also possible to apply a transform to the array elements before reduction.
The dedicated function `transform_reduce()` takes as input a monadic transform in addition to the reducer.
Again, builtin functions like `dot()` or `distance()` are available.

\snippet{trimleft} reduction_example.cpp transform

\section reduction-multiple Multi-array reductions

Function `transform_reduce()` accepts an arbitrary number of arrays.
In this case, the transform must take as many arguments as there are arrays.

\snippet{trimleft} reduction_example.cpp multiple

*/

}
